{"input_path":"/home/arc/pvl-dev/fixtures/formula.pdf","page_index":2,"page_count":3,"width":1224,"height":1584,"model_settings":{"use_doc_preprocessor":false,"use_layout_detection":true,"use_chart_recognition":false,"use_seal_recognition":false,"use_ocr_for_image_block":false,"format_block_content":false,"merge_layout_blocks":true,"markdown_ignore_labels":["number","footnote","header","header_image","footer","footer_image","aside_text"],"return_layout_polygon_points":true},"parsing_res_list":[{"block_label":"image","block_content":"","block_bbox":[103,165,584,795],"block_id":0,"block_order":null,"group_id":0,"block_polygon_points":[[103.0,165.0],[584.0,165.0],[584.0,795.0],[103.0,795.0]]},{"block_label":"figure_title","block_content":"Figure 7: Running example of ECo-DC","block_bbox":[189,820,505,842],"block_id":1,"block_order":null,"group_id":1,"block_polygon_points":[[189.0,820.0],[505.0,820.0],[505.0,842.0],[189.0,842.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Example 4.1: Here, we apply ECo-DC on the graph G in Figure 2 as an example. Figure 7 indicates the whole running process of ECo-DC on G, where the top-most part is G itself. The degeneracy is  $ \\delta(G) = 4 $. Then, we compute the steiner connectivities of all edges of G by invoking Compute-DC with input G and  $ [L, H] = [1, 4] $. Here,  $ GS_1^4(G) $ is the same as G. As  $ L \\neq H $ and  $ \\left\\lceil \\frac{L + H}{2} \\right\\rceil = 3 $, we compute the 3-ECCs of G and obtain the subgraphs induced by  $ S_1 = \\{v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_9\\} $ and  $ S_2 = \\{v_{10}, \\ldots, v_{13}\\} $, respectively. Thus, we obtain the two graphs  $ GS_1^2(G) $ and  $ GS_3^4(G) $ as shown in the middle layer of Figure 7. The computation continues on these two graphs with intervals  $ [1, 2] $ and  $ [3, 4] $, respectively.","block_bbox":[101,860,594,1103],"block_id":2,"block_order":1,"group_id":2,"block_polygon_points":[[101.0,860.0],[594.0,860.0],[594.0,1103.0],[101.0,1103.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The graph  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{3}^{4}(G) $ is composed of the two 3-ECCs of G as shown in right part of the middle layer of Figure 7. We compute the 4-ECCs of  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{3}^{4}(G) $, and obtain the subgraph induced by vertices  $ \\{v_{1}, v_{2}, \\ldots, v_{5}\\} $. Thus, all edges among vertices  $ \\{v_{1}, v_{2}, \\ldots, v_{5}\\} $ have steiner connectivities 4 as indicated in  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{4}^{4}(G) $, while the other edges have steiner connectivities 3 as demonstrated in  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{3}^{3}(G) $.","block_bbox":[101,1104,592,1239],"block_id":3,"block_order":2,"group_id":3,"block_polygon_points":[[101.0,1104.0],[592.0,1104.0],[592.0,1239.0],[101.0,1239.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The graph  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{1}^{2}(G) $ is obtained by contracting each of  $ S_{1} $ and  $ S_{2} $ into a super-vertex as shown in the left part of the middle layer of Figure 7. In  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{1}^{2}(G) $, there are two parallel edges between  $ s_{1} $ and  $ s_{2} $, corresponding to edges  $ (v_{9}, v_{11}) $ and  $ (v_{5}, v_{12}) $, respectively. As  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{1}^{2}(G) $ is 2-edge connected, the steiner-connectivities of  $ (v_{9}, v_{11}) $ and  $ (v_{5}, v_{12}) $ are 2.","block_bbox":[102,1239,592,1371],"block_id":4,"block_order":3,"group_id":4,"block_polygon_points":[[102.0,1239.0],[592.0,1239.0],[592.0,1371.0],[102.0,1371.0]]},{"block_label":"paragraph_title","block_content":"4.2 Constructing the Hierarchy Tree","block_bbox":[631,169,1012,193],"block_id":5,"block_order":4,"group_id":5,"block_polygon_points":[[631.0,169.0],[1012.0,169.0],[1012.0,193.0],[631.0,193.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Given the steiner connectivities of all edges of a graph $G$, Algorithm 4 constructs the hierarchy tree of ECo-decomposition of $G$ in a bottom-up manner. The main idea is as follows. First, the hierarchy tree $\\mathcal{T}$ is initialized as a forest of singleton nodes. Then, for each edge $(u, v) \\in E(G)$ in non-increasing order regarding $sc(\\cdot, \\cdot)$, we identify the tree in $\\mathcal{T}$ (specifically, the root $r_u$ of the tree) containing $u$ and the tree (specifically, the root $v_r$ of the tree) containing $v$. If $u$ and $v$ are already in the same tree $(i.e., r_u = r_v)$, then we do nothing. Otherwise, we merge the two trees into one in $\\mathcal{T}$, with the root of this newly formed tree having weight $sc(u, v)$.","block_bbox":[630,200,1123,419],"block_id":6,"block_order":5,"group_id":6,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,200.0],[1123.0,200.0],[1123.0,419.0],[630.0,419.0]]},{"block_label":"algorithm","block_content":"Algorithm 4: ConstructHierarchy\n\nInput: A graph G with  $ sc(u, v) $ for each edge  $ (u, v) $\nOutput: The hierarchy tree of ECo-decomposition of G\n\n1 Initialize an empty hierarchy tree T;\n2 for each vertex  $ u \\in V(G) $ do Insert a singleton node u into T;\n3 for each edge  $ (u, v) \\in E(G) $ in non-increasing  $ sc(u, v) $ order do\n4 Let  $ r_u $ (resp  $ r_v $) be the root of the tree in T containing u (resp v);\n5 if  $ r_u = r_v $ then continue;\n6 else if both  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ are ECC nodes with weight  $ sc(u, v) $ then\n7 Merge  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ into a single ECC node;\n8 else if none of  $ r_u $ or  $ r_v $ is an ECC node with weight  $ sc(u, v) $ then\n9 Create a new ECC node in T with weight  $ sc(u, v) $, and add  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ as its children;\n10 else\n11 Without loss of generality, assume  $ r_u $ is an ECC node with weight  $ sc(u, v) $, and add  $ r_v $ as a child of  $ r_u $ in T;","block_bbox":[634,444,1111,844],"block_id":7,"block_order":6,"group_id":7,"block_polygon_points":[[634.0,444.0],[1111.0,444.0],[1111.0,844.0],[634.0,844.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The pseudocode of constructing the hierarchy tree is illustrated in Algorithm 4, denoted by ConstructHierarchy. The input of the algorithm is a graph  $ G $ with  $ sc(u, v) $ precomputed for each edge  $ (u, v) $. It first initializes an empty hierarchy tree (Line 1), and creates a single-node tree in  $ \\mathcal{T} $ for each vertex of  $ G $ (Line 2). Then, the trees in  $ \\mathcal{T} $ will be merged with each other to form ECC nodes in the hierarchy tree. For each edge  $ (u, v) \\in E(G) $ sorted by  $ sc(u, v) $ in non-increasing order (Line 3), the roots of the trees in  $ \\mathcal{T} $ containing node  $ u $ and node  $ v $ are found, represented by  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ respectively (Line 4). If  $ r_u = r_v $, it implies that vertices  $ u $ and  $ v $ have already been merged into the same tree so that the algorithm skips the current edge (Line 5); otherwise, the algorithm merges  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ into a single tree based on the following three cases. (1) If both  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ are ECC nodes with weight  $ sc(u, v) $, it merges  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ into a single ECC node (Lines 6–7). (2) If neither  $ r_u $ nor  $ r_v $ is an ECC node with weight  $ sc(u, v) $, it creates a new ECC node in  $ \\mathcal{T} $ with weight  $ sc(u, v) $ whose children are  $ r_u $ and  $ r_v $ (Lines 8–9). (3) The last situation is that one of  $ r_u $ or  $ r_v $ is an ECC node with weight  $ sc(u, v) $ and the other is not; note that, if the other one is an ECC node, then its weight must be larger than  $ sc(u, v) $. Assume that  $ r_u $ is the one with weight  $ sc(u, v) $,  $ r_v $ would be added as a child of  $ r_u $. Similar steps would be applied to the situation where  $ r_v $ is the one with weight  $ sc(u, v) $ (Lines 10–11).","block_bbox":[625,877,1122,1377],"block_id":8,"block_order":7,"group_id":8,"block_polygon_points":[[625.0,877.0],[1122.0,877.0],[1122.0,1377.0],[625.0,1377.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The most time-consuming operation in Algorithm 4 is Line 4, which aims to find the root of the tree that contains a node u.","block_bbox":[630,1377,1122,1422],"block_id":9,"block_order":8,"group_id":9,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,1377.0],[1122.0,1377.0],[1122.0,1422.0],[630.0,1422.0]]},{"block_label":"number","block_content":"1151","block_bbox":[588,1507,635,1528],"block_id":10,"block_order":null,"group_id":10,"block_polygon_points":[[588.0,1507.0],[634.0,1507.0],[634.0,1527.0],[588.0,1527.0]]}],"layout_det_res":{"input_path":null,"page_index":null,"boxes":[{"cls_id":14,"label":"image","score":0.9370412826538086,"coordinate":[103,165,584,795],"order":null,"polygon_points":[[103.0,165.0],[584.0,165.0],[584.0,795.0],[103.0,795.0]]},{"cls_id":7,"label":"figure_title","score":0.8414303660392761,"coordinate":[189,820,505,842],"order":null,"polygon_points":[[189.0,820.0],[505.0,820.0],[505.0,842.0],[189.0,842.0]]},{"cls_id":22,"label":"text","score":0.9241834282875061,"coordinate":[101,860,594,1103],"order":1,"polygon_points":[[101.0,860.0],[594.0,860.0],[594.0,1103.0],[101.0,1103.0]]},{"cls_id":15,"label":"inline_formula","score":0.3787505328655243,"coordinate":[376,973,433,991],"order":2,"polygon_points":[[376.0,973.0],[376.0,990.0],[432.0,990.0],[432.0,973.0]]},{"cls_id":15,"label":"inline_formula","score":0.719704270362854,"coordinate":[472,971,557,995],"order":3,"polygon_points":[[472.0,971.0],[556.0,971.0],[556.0,994.0],[472.0,994.0]]},{"cls_id":15,"label":"inline_formula","score":0.3290458917617798,"coordinate":[123,928,189,947],"order":4,"polygon_points":[[123.0,928.0],[188.0,928.0],[188.0,946.0],[123.0,946.0]]},{"cls_id":15,"label":"inline_formula","score":0.7507834434509277,"coordinate":[106,1017,246,1037],"order":5,"polygon_points":[[106.0,1017.0],[245.0,1017.0],[245.0,1036.0],[106.0,1036.0]]},{"cls_id":15,"label":"inline_formula","score":0.7512619495391846,"coordinate":[283,1017,416,1037],"order":6,"polygon_points":[[283.0,1017.0],[415.0,1017.0],[415.0,1036.0],[283.0,1036.0]]},{"cls_id":15,"label":"inline_formula","score":0.6176038384437561,"coordinate":[272,1038,325,1060],"order":7,"polygon_points":[[272.0,1038.0],[324.0,1038.0],[324.0,1059.0],[272.0,1059.0]]},{"cls_id":22,"label":"text","score":0.924185574054718,"coord